\abstract{}

\keywords{Interpersonal communication, disagreements, polarization, persuasion, attitude change, behavior change, intellectual humility, implementation intentions}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

We spend a remarkable amount of effort avoiding disagreements with the people around us. We steer conversations away from contentious topics, soften our opinions, and sometimes remain silent rather than risk a fight. Yet disagreement is also one of the few occasions on which another person explicitly presents us with a view that differs from our own. And when that person is someone we know and care about, the encounter combines counter-attitudinal information with a socially meaningful source of influence. 

This creates a tension around disagreements with known others (DKOs). The conversations we are most inclined to avoid may be among those with the greatest potential to help us understand other viewpoints and motivate us to critically reflect on our existing attitudes and behaviors. Unlike encountering an opposing view from a stranger or an institution, one from a close other is harder to dismiss: psychological closeness amplifies social influence, making the response of close others more likley to be spontaneously represented and incorporated into our own judgments and behavior \parencite{smithInfluenceRepresentationsOthers2016}. At the same time, the established relationship that makes disagreement potentially influential can make it more difficult to navigate, potentially threatening relational harmony or escalating into conflict that impacts social relationships beyond a single conversation. 

These dynamics matter because many of the beliefs guiding consequential decisions are formed and maintained in everyday social relationships. Societally-relevant DKOs - those concerning health, politics, or the environment-can therefore do more than determine who wins and argument. They may provide opportunities to encounter and consider perspectives that challenge existing beliefs, deepen understanding of why others think differently, or instead reinforce division or strain relationships. Among the large variety of societally-relevant issues, we focus operationally on a manageable subset, namely those related to health, politics, or the environment. Understanding when societally-relevant DKOs occur-and when they become constructive rather than counterproductive-may thus illuminate an underappreciated pathway of interpersonal influence with implications for both individual attitude/behavior change and social cohesion. Here, we present two pre-registered, empirical studies addressing a core research question (RQ):

RQ: Can we systematically increase the frequency and quality of societally-relevant DKOs with predictable outcomes?

In Study 1, we surveyed a large, representative sample of the Dutch population to establish when and how societally-relevant DKOs occur naturally, who engages in them, and what outcomes they are associated with. In Study 2, we test two interventions designed to reinforce natural tendencies identified in Study 1 that are linked to high quality DKOs with predictable outcomes in a longitudinal, online experiment.

\subsection{Theoretical Background Study 1}

Several rich traditions examine the nature and effects of interpersonal conversations in general, and interpersonal conflict or disagreement, specifically. In what follows, we briefly review key, interdisciplinary evidence that helps characterize the frequency, context, quality, and outcomes of societally-relevant DKOs, and derive several pre-registered hypotheses. By quality, we mean the deliberative nature of the conversation, operationalized based on the Perceived Discourse Quality Index (PDQI) \parencite{caluwaertsDoesInterGroupDeliberation2014} which includes indicators such as the extent to which the conversation was perceived as respectful, open, and honest. Further, we consider a broad range of outcomes related to mutual understanding (e.g., relational consequences, learning) and change (attitudes and behavior). 

Our literature review highlights two overarching knowledge gaps. First, research on the frequency of DKOs is largely disconnected from work on the quality and outcomes. Frequency is typically studied using observational methods which highlight a preference for engaging with known others. Work on quality and outcomes tends to be experimental and favor controlled interactions between strangers. This disconnect complicates both the construction of a holistic understanding of DKOs and the safe design of interventions: increasing frequency without knowing the consequences for quality and outcomes can easily backfire, and costly quality-focused interventions are wasted if the conversations they target rarely occur. Second, empirical data are siloed within disconnected topic areas - marital conflict \parencite{kanterDoesCoupleCommunication2022}, organizational knowledge exchange \parencite{vanwoerkomRomanceLearningDisagreement2010}, cross-partisan political conversations \parencite{combsReducingPoliticalPolarization2023a}, health communication \parencite{hendriksWhatDutchCollege2015}, and environmental peer persuasion \parencite{makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017}-making individual findings difficult to generalize or compare across contexts. 

\subsubsection{Frequency}

If DKOs are a potential catalyst of social cohesion and change, it is important to understand how often they occur and who is most likely to engage in them. Recent US-centric evidence suggests that conversations about health and politics occur with some regularity, while conversations about climate and the environment are rarer. Atari and colleagues \citeyear{atariPaucityMoralityEveryday2023} found that participants estimated 20.1\% of all their interactions concerned health and 17.5\% politics (also see \parencite{keskinturkWhatAreYou2025}), whereas Latkin and colleauges \citeyear{latkinTheyTalkWeather2024} observed that over 60\% of their large sample had not discussed climate change with friends or family in the previous month. Directly comparable cross-topic data remain largely absent. 

Data on the frequency of disagreements specifically are sparser still, and concentrated mainly in the political domain. The available evidence suggests that disagreements are generally infrequent and occur predominantly with known others rather than strangers \parencite{hofstraImpactDyadsExtended2026, makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017, jostCognitiveMotivationalMechanisms2022, hendriksWhatDutchCollege2015, moreyWhoMattersTypes2012}. For instance, Schmitt-Beck and Schnaudt \citeyear{schmitt-beckEverydayPoliticalTalk2023} found that disagreements with known others about politics in Germany occur "sometimes" and the Survey Center on American Life \parencite{soal} found that 58\% of US Americans disagreed about politics with a friend or family member in the previous year while only 34\% had done so with a stranger. 

Several mechanisms likely suppress disagreement frequency. A well-documented human aversion to counter-attitudinal content-manifest in convfirmation bias, selective exposure, selective sharing (CITE XINYAO AS SUMBITTED), and motivated reasoning (CITE XINYAO)-reduces the likelihood of seeking out opposing views. Beyond information-processing tendencies, the social stakes of conversations with known others activate belonging motives \parencite{baumeisterNeedBelongDesire1995} that are not optimally served by initiating a disagreement, which may 
be perceived as relational threat. Consistent with this, people systematically underestimate others' receptiveness to opposing political views and expect conversations with those who disagree to be more negative than they turn out to be, leading them to avoid such exchanges \parencite{mataPeopleUnderestimateHow2026}. Pluralistic ignorance-the mistaken belief that others do not share one's concern about, for example, climate change-constitutdes an additional barrier GEIGER SWIM 2016. When counter-attitudinal conversations do occur, they can produce frustration and sometimes backfire, with attitudes polarizing rather than converging \parencite{makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017, jostCognitiveMotivationalMechanisms2022}. 

Here, we quantify and directly compare the frequency of DKOs across three societally-relevant topics and hypothesize:

H1: The majority of DKOs occurs with known rather than unknown others. 

Less is known about what predicts DKO frequency at the individual level. Schmitt-Beck and Schnaudt \citeyear{schmitt-beckEverydayPoliticalTalk2023} identify conflict-seeking disposition and political topic interest as positive predictors of political disagreements. More generally, research on conversation initiation and information sharing shows that self-relevant, socially relevant and emotionally charged content is communicated more frequently \parencite{scholzMediaContentSharing2020, cosmePerceivedSelfSocial2025a, bergerWhatGetsShared2025}. While self-relevance supports the role of topic interest, emotionality may be particularly important for disagreements: strong feelings about a topic can override the social costs of raising it, suggesting that many DKOs arise spontaneously rather than by design. We therefore hypothesize: 

H2: Those with higher tolerance for disagreement report more frequent DKOs.; and
H3: Higher interest in and knowledge about a topic is positively associated with the frequency of DKOs about that topic. 
H4: At the beginning of the conversation, the average DKO is characterized by a stronger negative rather than positive emotional atmosphere. 
H5: The majority of DKOs is unplanned. 

\subsubsection{Conversational Motives \& Quality}

When DKOs do occur, the motives that drive them and the actual characteristics of the interaction are crucial in determining the outcomes of an individual DKO as well as the likelihood of reoccurrence as has been highlighted in interdisciplinary (experimental) work (CITE CONFLICT STYLES AND OTHERS). A key limitation of this literature, however, is that experimental work predominantly involves interactions between strangers or between experts and laypersons (e.g., \cite{creativeTalkingClimateHandbook,binnquistZoomSolutionPromoting2022, hendriksInterveningConversationsHow2020,speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026}. This matters for two reasons: the natural communication dynamics of established relationships differ from those of stranger interactions, and people's capacity to authentically adopt experimentally assigned motives or strategies may be limited when conversation partners are familiar with their natural approach. Establishing the baseline distribution of motives and conversation quality in naturally occurring DKOs is therefore a necessary first step for identifying where interventions targeting DKOsare most needed and most likely to succeed. 

RQ1: What are common motives of DKO participants?

RQ2: What are the characteristics of DKOs?

Contrasting the literatures on DKO frequency and quality reveals a further tension: the conditions that make DKOs likely-emotionally charged, unplanned encounters-are not necessarily those conducive to high-quality discourse. Emotions increase the probability of initiatying a conversation but may impair careful listening and promote motivated reasoning. Social cohesion motives drive interaction, but DKOs require a tolerance for conflict that cohesion motives may actively suppress. This suggests that the predictors of DKO frequncy and the predictors of DKO quality may partially dissciate, aming outcomes diffuctu to anticipate without empirical data. We hypothesize: 

H6: Planned vs. unplanned DKOs are characterized by weaker negative emotions (H1A) and higher discourse quality (H1B). 
H7: DKOs that are characterized by a more negative emotional atmosphere at the beginning of the conversation are also characterized by lower discourse quality.
H8: Higher intellectual humility is associated with higher discourse quality during DKOs. 

\subsubsection{Outcomes}

Societally-relevant DKOs can have important consequences for both individuals and, in aggregate, for society. We focus on two classes of outcomes: those relevant to social cohesion through mutual understanding and relational quality, and those related to attitude and behavior change.

There is a strong theoretical basis for expecting interactions with close others to be impactful: work on the RICOR model \parencite{smithInfluenceRepresentationsOthers2016} proposes that responses from psychologically close others are spontaneously represented and incorporated into one's own judgments, providing an automatic mechanism for social influence that does not depend on deliberate conformity motives. Longitudinal political-network research corroborates this, showing tha tdisagreement and partner selection are intertwined and that friends and family exert meaningufl influence on political attitudes over time \parencite{belloInfluenceMightierSelection2014}. Health psychology and communication scholars have similarly demonstrated the influence of naturally occurring interpersonal conversations on health and environmental behaviors \parencite{hendriksInterveningConversationsHow2020, makiEnvironmentalPeerPersuasion2017}, though disagreements specifically remain understudied. 

Despite this theoretical potential, disagreements are widely anticipated as negative experiences. Wald, Kardas, and Epley \citeyear{waldMisplacedDividesDiscussing2024} found across three experiments that people systematically underestimate how positive disagreements about divisive topics will be. Experimental studies of conversations between strangers generally confirm this mismatch, with actual experiences rated more positively than expected \parencite{binnquistZoomSolutionPromoting2022}. Whether the same holds for interactions with known others is less clear: relational stakes are higher, but so is familiarity, and expectations may be better calibrated to prior experience. Importantly, outcomes need not be uniform across dimensions. De Jong \citeyear{dejongCrosspartisanDiscussionsReduced2024} showed that brief cross-partisan conversations increased out-party sympathy and willingness to engage in future discussions without changing core policy positions, with the sympathy effect attenuated when issue disagreement was greater. Combs and colleagues \citeyear{combsReducingPoliticalPolarization2023a} similarly found that actual cross-party conversations produced substantial reductions in affective polarization, with reductions tied to the civility of the interaction. Together, these findings underscore that relational and attitudinal outcomes can dissociate, and that both must be measured and targeted.

While we have reasonable data on expected outcomes and on outcomes of experimentally manipulted conversations, the actual distribution of outcomes of naturally occurring DKOs across topics remains largely unknown. We therefore pose:

RQ 3: What are the outcomes of DKOs?

Assuming that people's expectations are calibrated—at least in part—by their actual experiences with known others, we predict:

H9: On average, DKOs are perceived as unenjoyable (H1A), are unlikely to change attitudes (H1B) and behavior (H1C), and do not motivate repetition of similar conversations (H1D). 

\subsubsection{Predicting Outcomes}

Relatively little is known about what predicts the outcomes of naturally occurring disagreements. The existing experimental literature is almost exclusively focused on politics and on two levers: conversation quality and conversational motives. Cross-topic comparisons and naturalistic relationships between conversation context, participant characteristics, and outcomes remain largely unexamined. We therefore ask:

RQ4: What are the effects of DKO context, participant characteristics, conversational motives, and conversation characteristics on DKO outcomes? 

Discourse quality. Argyle and colleagues \citeyear{argyleAIChatAssistants2023} showed that AI-assisted real-time recommendations improved perceived understanding, reduced divisiveness, and improved conversational tone-without shifting policy attitudes. De Jong \citeyear{dejongCrosspartisanDiscussionsReduced2024} identified perspective-taking, feelings of inclusion, perceived commonality, and reduced misperceptions as predictors of conversational outcomes. Similarly, Speer and colleagues \citeyear{speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026} found that exploration and openness during conversations predicted agreement. Santoro and colleagues \citeyear{santoroListenChangeLongitudinal2025} found that high-quality, non-judgmental listening increased interpersonal perceptions and reduced defensiveness. These findings converge on the conclusion that higher discourse quality consistently predicts better relational and experiential outcomes, even when attitudinal change is absent: 

H10: DKOs with higher discourse quality are perceived to be more instructive (H1A), more enjoyable (H1B), less likely to harm the relationship of the conversation partners (H1C) and associated with higher willingness to have similar conversations in the future (H1D). 

Conversational motives. The evidence here is more equivocal. Nussbaum \citeyear{nussbaumEffectGoalInstructions2005} found that a persuasion goal generates more adversarial communication than an exploration goal. Speer and colleagues \citeyear{speerFindingAgreementFunctional2026} found that a compromise goal produced more agreement than a persuasion goal. DeMarree and colleagues \citeyear{demarreeLearningGoalsListening2026} found that a goal to listen to a disagreeing stranger rather than to persuade increased perceived psychological safety and, thereby, reduced polarization. Santoro and colleagues \citeyear{santoroListenChangeLongitudinal2025} manipulated high-quality listening in trained canvassers showing that listening improved interpersonal outcomes, but not persuasion. Together, these findings suggest that conversational motives are important, but have only examined the role of experimentally manipulated motives in pairs focusing on conversations with strangers. The effects of naturally occurring motives in DKOs reamin an open question we address empirically. 

Planning and emotional onset. When people disagree with known others, they can draw on a shared history and a deep understanding of the relationship. A planned conversation allows them to leverage this knowledge to calibrate their approach; an emotionally charged, unplanned onset is likely to undermine it. We therefore hypothesize:

H11: Planned vs. unplanned DKOs are perceived to be more instructive (H2A) more enjoyable (H2B), less likely to harm the relationship of the conversation partners (H2C), more likely to lead to attitude (H2D) and behavior change (H2E), and more likely to be repeated (H2F). 

H12: Stronger negative emotions at the onset of DKOs are associated with lower enjoyability (H3A), higher perceived harm the relationship of the conversation partners (H3B), lower likelihood of attitude (H3C) and behavior change (H3D), and lower repetition likelihood (H3E). RQ9 - Topics: What are similarities and differences between topics (health, environment, and politics)?

Finally, because this is the first study to examine DKOs across health, politics, and environmental topics in a single representative sample, we treat cross-topic comparisons as exploratory:

RQ5: What are the similarities and differences between DKOs across health, political, and environmental topics?

\section{Methods Study 1}

After providing informed consent (ERB # ANONYMIZED), participants completed an online survey consisting of two main parts: In Part 1, participants were asked to recall details about one specific DKO. Participants were randomly assigned to one of the three focal topics (health, politics, or the environment). Participants who were not able to recall a DKO about the assigned topic where first asked to recall a DKO about another focal topic. Participants who did not recall any societally-relevant DKO, were asked to recall a DKO about any topic. In Part 2, participants were asked to report experiences with and impressions of societally-relevant DKOs in general. The pre-registration, measures, and analysis scripts are available on our OSF page. 

