\subsubsection{2.2.2 Messages}

We generated $\Sexpr{length(unique(study1a_drink_df$QualtricsMsgID))}$ alcohol-related messages ($\Sexpr{study1a_drink_df %>% group_by(condition) %>% summarize(n_msgs=length(unique(QualtricsMsgID))) %>% summarize(first(n_msgs))}$ per Valence: pro/anti-alcohol x Source:
professional/peer-produced condition). This large message set enhances the representativeness of our results and minimizes case-category confounds \parencite{kim_reliable_2019}. 

A convenience sample of images was purposefully sampled from public Instagram posts and prior research. Pro- and anti-alcohol images portrayed alcohol (consumption) positively or negatively, respectively. Professional messages were sampled from Instagram pages of businesses (e.g., alcohol brands) and international, English-language anti-(binge) drinking campaigns studied in prior research \parencite{scholz_neural_2019}. Peer-produced messages were sampled from public Instagram accounts of ’normal’ users. Images were chosen to highlight excessive consumption (e.g., exuberant situations like parties, consumption of high-proof alcohol or large quantities, or displays of consequences of excessive consumption). Hashtags were manipulated to reinforce condition (e.g., using brand names, campaign slogans, or commonly used hashtags) and to balance the amount of text across conditions. Stimuli were embedded in templates resembling Instagram posts (Supplementary Figure S1).

\subsubsection{2.2.3 Procedure}

Each participant viewed $\Sexpr{study1a_drink_df %>% group_by(pIDs) %>% count() %>% ungroup() %>% summarize(unique(n)[1])}$ randomly chosen messages, implementing a 2 Valence (pro vs. anti-alcohol) x 2 Source (professional vs. peer-produced) full-factorial design. After each message exposure, participants rated their current alcohol craving, manipulation check items (see Supplementary Materials), emotional responses, and relatability. 

\subsection{2.3 Study 1b}

\subsubsection{2.3.1 Participants}

Participants (N = $\Sexpr{dim(ppts_study1b)[1]}$) were recruited according to the Study 1a procedure (descriptive statistics in Table 1). 

\subsubsection{2.3.2 Messages}

We supplemented the Study 1a message sample by generating $\Sexpr{study1b_drink_df %>% filter(grepl('non',condition)) %>% summarize(length(unique(QualtricsURL)))}$ non-alcoholic messages (15/source condition) mirroring the Study 1a message sampling and design procedures. Non-alcoholic messages depicted (the consumption of) non-alcoholic beverages positively and were matched to the Study 1a alcoholic messages in that they depicted exuberant situations like dancing and large groups laughing or depictions of large beverage quantities.

\subsubsection{2.3.3 Procedure}

Each participant viewed $\Sexpr{study1b_drink_df %>% group_by(pIDs) %>% count() %>% ungroup() %>% summarize(unique(n)[1])}$ randomly chosen messages from the full samples of alcoholic ($\Sexpr{study1b_drink_df %>% filter(!grepl('non',condition)) %>% group_by(pIDs) %>% count() %>% ungroup() %>% summarize(unique(n)[1])}$/person) and non-alcoholic ($\Sexpr{study1b_drink_df %>% filter(grepl('non',condition)) %>% group_by(pIDs) %>% count() %>% ungroup() %>% summarize(unique(n)[1])}$/person) messages, implementing a 3 Valence (pro vs. anti vs. non-alcoholic) x 2 Source (professional vs. peer-produced) full-factorial design. As in Study 1a, participants rated their current alcohol craving, manipulation check items (see Supplementary Materials), emotional responses, and relatability after each message exposure.

\subsection{2.4 Study 2}

\subsubsection{2.4.1 Participants}

Participants (N = $\Sexpr{length(unique(study2_logs$pID))}$) meeting the eligibility criteria (age >=18, has “experiences with drinking alcohol in your life”) were recruited from our university’s student subject pool (descriptive statistics in Table 1). 

\subsubsection{2.4.2 Messages}

A subset of $\Sexpr{study2_logs %>% group_by(pID) %>% count() %>% ungroup() %>% summarize(unique(n)[1])}$ messages (N = $\Sexpr{study2_logs %>% group_by(pID,condition) %>% count() %>% ungroup() %>% summarize(unique(n)[1])}$ per valence * source condition) of alcohol-related messages used in Study 1a/b served as stimuli in Study 2. To answer an orthogonal, exploratory research question, we generated two versions of each message, messages designed as Instagram posts as used in Studies 1a/b and messages designed to resemble Google search results with text as search terms instead of hashtags. Because media context effects were not significant, we collapse across platform types.

\subsubsection{2.4.3 Procedure}

Serving as conceptual replication of Studies 1a/b and pilot study for follow-up neuroimaging work, Study 2 employed an automatically paced task optimized for the functional neuroimaging environment. Each participant completed $\Sexpr{study2_logs %>% group_by(pID) %>% count() %>% ungroup() %>% summarize(unique(n)[1])}$ trials (Supplementary Figure 2), consisting of message exposure (5 s), brief fixation period (a blank screen with a centered cross which participants were asked to focus on while resting briefly), standardized beverage cue and craving rating (image of alcoholic -  $\Sexpr{study2_logs %>% filter(pID!='434970_study2') %>% group_by(pID,type) %>% count() %>% filter(type=='alcohol') %>% ungroup() %>%summarize(min(n))}$-$\Sexpr{study2_logs %>% filter(pID!='434970_study2') %>% group_by(pID,type) %>% count() %>% filter(type=='alcohol') %>% ungroup() %>%summarize(max(n))}$ trials/person - or non-alcohol - $\Sexpr{study2_logs %>% filter(pID!='434970_study2') %>% group_by(pID,type) %>% count() %>% filter(type=='non-alcohol') %>% ungroup() %>%summarize(min(n))}$-$\Sexpr{study2_logs %>% filter(pID!='434970_study2') %>% group_by(pID,type) %>% count() %>% filter(type=='non-alcohol') %>% ungroup() %>%summarize(max(n))}$ trials/person – beverages; 4 s), between-trial fixation. Standardized (non-)alcohol cues were sampled from the Amsterdam Beverage Picture Set (Pronk et al., 2015). After the first two seconds of cue exposure, participants rated their craving for the beverage displayed by the cue. 

As such, this task implemented a 2 Valence (pro vs. anti-alcohol) x 2 Source (professional vs. peer-produced) x 2 Cue type (alcoholic vs. non-alcoholic) design.

\subsection{2.5 Self-Report Measures}

In Studies 1a/b, craving was measured immediately after message exposure: “Looking at this post makes me want to drink alcohol today.” (Likert-type scale: 1 = Not at all, 7 = Very much). In Study 2, craving was assessed after exposure to (non-)alcoholic beverages cues: “Imagine holding this drink. Rate: [Looking at this post] makes me want to drink this” (Likert-type scale: 1 = Not at all, 5 = Very much).

Emotional responses were assessed in Studies 1a/b with two items: “To what extent does this post make you feel the following?” [positive/negative] (Likert-type scale: 1 = Not at all, 7 = Very much).

Relatability was assessed with two items in Studies 1a/b: “To what extent do you agree or disagree with the following statements? This post … " [shows people that are like me/shows a situation I'm familiar with] (Likert-type scale: 1 = Totally disagree, 7 = Totally agree). Supplementary Table 1 reports descriptive statistics.

Drinking and binge drinking frequency were assessed using two items based on the NIAAA's “Task Force on Recommended Alcohol Questions” (adjusted for European drink sizes; \cite{niaaa_recommended_2003}) to minimize survey length for the online panel participants recruited in Study 1a/b, and using the longer AUDIT \parencite{saunders_development_1993} for the university sample in Study 2.

\subsection{2.6 Data Analysis}

<<echo=FALSE>>=
study1ab_fam=bind_rows(study1a_familiarity_df,study1b_familiarity_df)
cor=study1ab_fam %>% filter(grepl('familiar',familiarity_variable) | grepl('like me',familiarity_variable)) %>% 
  mutate(familiarity_variable=ifelse(grepl('familiar',familiarity_variable),'familiar','like_me')) %>%
  pivot_wider(values_from=fam_rating,names_from=familiarity_variable) %>% 
  summarize(pc=cor(like_me,familiar)) %>% pull(pc)
@

A relatability index was created by averaging the “like me” and situational familiarity ratings per trial (correlation of item scores: r = $\Sexpr{round(cor,2)}$). Because craving scales differed across studies, craving was z-scored in analyses including data from both Study 1 and 2.

Because the three datasets provide several opportunities for (conceptual) replication and to pool data across studies to enhance statistical power, we analyzed data by RQ or H rather than by study. The within-subjects data were analyzed using multi-level regression models as implemented in R's lmerTest package \parencite{kuznetsova_lmertest_2019}, accounting for nesting within participants, messages, and, where applicable, studies. Marginal means and post-hoc pairwise comparisons were computed using R's emmeans package\parencite{lenth_emmeans_2022}. Model equations are reported in relevant tables or the results section.

\subsection{2.7 Power and Replicability}

Per study, trial numbers per person were chosen for optimal participant burden and to guarantee sufficient independent ratings per message to generate valid message evaluations (\cite{kim_efficient_2019}; Study 1a: M = $\Sexpr{round(s1a$mean,2)}$, SD = $\Sexpr{round(s1a$sd,2)}$; Study 1b: M = $\Sexpr{round(s1b_a$mean,2)}$, SD = $\Sexpr{round(s1b_a$sd,2)}$ for alcoholic messages, which were already evaluated in Study 1a, M = 24.53, SD = 4.14 for non-alcoholic messages; Study 2: all messages rated by all $\Sexpr{length(unique(study2_logs$pID))}$ participants).

% Power analyses are available in scripts/power.R

We confirmed statistical power post-hoc using simR (1000 simulations) for the most complex effects of interest. Study 2 was powered to detect small interaction effects of conditions on craving (beta=0.2, power= 81.70\%, 95\%CI[79.16, 84.05]). Studies 1 a/b, while underpowered alone, support these Study 2 effects through conceptual replication. Analyses concerning emotions and relatability, pooling data across Studies 1 a/b, were well powered to detect small interactions between condition and mechanism on craving (beta=0.2, power = 100.0\%, 95\%CI[99.63, 100.0]).

\section{3. Results}

Manipulation checks in Study 1a/b confirmed that the stimuli were perceived as intended according to the valence and source manipulations (see Supplementary Materials). 

\subsection{3.1 RQ1: Valence and source effects on alcohol craving}

<<emmeansOneB,echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
mm_valence1b=read_csv('Tables/craving_table_emmeans_valence_1b.csv',col_types=cols())
pc_valence1b=read_csv('Tables/craving_table_pairwiseComps_valence1b.csv',col_types=cols())
pc_pronon_1b=pc_valence1b %>% filter(contrast=='(non-alcoholic) - (pro-alcohol)') %>% mutate(estimate=paste0('estimate = ', round(estimate,2), ', SE= ', round(SE,2), ', p ', formatp(p.value))) %>% .$estimate
pc_antinon_1b=pc_valence1b %>% filter(contrast=='(anti-alcohol) - (non-alcoholic)') %>% mutate(estimate=paste0('estimate = ', round(estimate,2), ', SE= ', round(SE,2), ', p ', formatp(p.value))) %>% .$estimate
@

First, we examined relative effects of message valence (pro- vs. non- vs. anti-alcohol) on alcohol craving using Study 1b data (the only study including non-alcoholic messages). Results show a significant influence of valence on craving (Table \ref{tab:CravingTable}, Figure \ref{fig:cravingPlots}A). Pro-alcohol messages ($M_{marginal}$ = $\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$emmean[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='pro-alcohol')],2)}$, 95\% CI[$\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$lower.CL[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='pro-alcohol')],2)}$,$\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$upper.CL[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='pro-alcohol')],2)}$])  significantly enhanced standardized alcohol craving relative to non-alcoholic messages ($M_{marginal}$ = $\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$emmean[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='non-alcoholic')],2)}$, 95\% CI[$\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$lower.CL[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='non-alcoholic')],2)}$,$\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$upper.CL[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='non-alcoholic')],2)}$]; $\Sexpr{pc_pronon_1b}$). The effect of anti- ($M_{marginal}$ = $\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$emmean[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='anti-alcohol')],2)}$, 95\% CI[$\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$lower.CL[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='anti-alcohol')],2)}$,$\Sexpr{round(mm_valence1b$upper.CL[which(mm_valence1b$val_cond=='anti-alcohol')],2)}$])  compared to non-alcoholic messages was substantially smaller and not significantly different from zero ($\Sexpr{pc_antinon_1b}$). Table 2 shows that the strong valence effect (pro- > anti-alcohol) on alcohol craving replicates across all three studies.

<<CravingTable, echo=FALSE,results='asis'>>=
CravingTable=read_csv('Tables/cravingTable.csv',col_types=cols()) 

CravingTable[is.na(CravingTable)]<-''

kable(CravingTable,caption='Condition Effects on Standardized Craving (B [95\\% CI], p-value)',booktabs=T,digit=2, format='latex') %>%
 kable_styling(latex_options = "scale_down") %>% add_footnote("Note. pID = participant ID; sID = stimulus ID; Model equations: Study 1a & b: Standardized alcohol craving ~ valence * source + (1|participant) + (1|stimulus), where valence has two categories (pro/anti-alcohol) in Study 1a and an additional third category (non-alcoholic) in Study 1b. Study 2: Standardized craving (alcoholic and non-alcoholic) ~ valence * source + valence * cue type + source * cue type + (1|participant) + (1|stimulus)")
@

<<cravingPlots,fig.cap="Average Beverage Craving by Stimulus Valence and Binge Drinking Habits; A. Alcohol craving in Studies 1a/b by valence condition, B. Alcohol and non-alcoholic craving in Study 2 by valence condition, C. Alcohol craving in Study 1b by valence condition and binge drinking frequency. Error bars represent standard errors.", echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=

study1a_data=study1a_drink_df %>% group_by(QualtricsMsgID) %>%
    summarize(avg_drink_rating=mean(drink_rating,na.rm=T),val_cond=unique(val_cond)) %>%
             group_by(val_cond) %>% 
             summarize(se_drink_rating=std.error(avg_drink_rating),
                       avg_drink_rating=mean(avg_drink_rating,na.rm=T)) %>% ungroup() %>%
    mutate(Study='Study 1a')

study1b_data=study1b_drink_df %>% group_by(filename) %>%
    summarize(avg_drink_rating=mean(drink_rating,na.rm=T),val_cond=unique(val_cond)) %>%
             group_by(val_cond) %>% 
             summarize(se_drink_rating=std.error(avg_drink_rating),
                       avg_drink_rating=mean(avg_drink_rating,na.rm=T)) %>% ungroup() %>%
    mutate(Study='Study 1b')

valPlot1_data=rbind(study1a_data,study1b_data)

valPlot1= valPlot1_data%>% 
  mutate(val_cond=factor(val_cond,levels=c('anti-alcohol','non-alcoholic','pro-alcohol')),
         Study=factor(Study,levels=c('Study 1a','Study 1b'))) %>%
  ggplot(aes(x=val_cond,y=avg_drink_rating,fill=Study)) + 
  geom_col(position='dodge') + 
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=avg_drink_rating-(1.96*se_drink_rating), ymax=avg_drink_rating+(1.96*se_drink_rating)),width=.2,position=position_dodge(width=0.9)) + ggtitle('Study 1a/b (Craving scale: 1-7)') + 
  ylab('Average Beverage Craving') + 
  xlab('Valence Condition') + 
  labs(tag='A')+
  theme_bw() + 
  theme(legend.position='bottom',text=element_text(size=10)) +
  scale_y_continuous(breaks=seq(1,7,1),limits=c(0,7)) + 
  scale_fill_manual(values=c('lightgrey','darkgrey'))

# New facet label names for type variable
type.labs <- c("alcoholic cues", "non-alcoholic cues")
names(type.labs) <- c("alcohol", "non-alcohol")

valPlot2=study2_logs %>% group_by(file,val_cond,type) %>% 
  summarize(avg_rating.keys=mean(rating.keys,na.rm=T)) %>%
  group_by(val_cond,type) %>% 
  summarize(avg_drink_rating=mean(avg_rating.keys),
            se_drink_rating=std.error(avg_rating.keys,na.rm=T)) %>% ungroup() %>% 
         ggplot(aes(val_cond,avg_drink_rating)) + geom_bar(stat='identity',fill='darkgrey') + geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=avg_drink_rating-(1.96*se_drink_rating), ymax=avg_drink_rating+(1.96*se_drink_rating)),width=.2) + ggtitle('Study 2 (Craving Scale 1-5)') + ylab('Average Beverage Craving (1-5)') + xlab('Valence Condition') + labs(y='',tag='B') + facet_wrap(~type,labeller=labeller(type=type.labs)) + theme_bw() + theme(legend.position='bottom',text=element_text(size=10)) + scale_y_continuous(breaks=seq(1,5,1),limits=c(0,5))

bingePlot=study1b_drink_df %>% 
  left_join(ppts_study1b) %>%
  mutate(binge_drinking_rev=case_when(binge_drinking==10~1,
                                      binge_drinking==9~2,
                                      binge_drinking==8~3,
                                      binge_drinking==7~4,
                                      binge_drinking==6~5,
                                      binge_drinking==5~6,
                                      binge_drinking==4~7,
                                      binge_drinking==3~8,
                                      binge_drinking==2~9,
                                      binge_drinking==1~10,
                                      TRUE~binge_drinking),
        binge_drink_cat=case_when(binge_drinking_rev>median(binge_drinking_rev)~'>Median',
                                  binge_drinking_rev<=median(binge_drinking_rev)~'<=Median',
                                  TRUE~NA_character_))%>%
    group_by(val_cond,binge_drink_cat) %>%
    summarize(se_drink_rating=std.error(drink_rating),
              drink_rating=mean(drink_rating,na.rm=T)) %>% ungroup() %>%
  ggplot(aes(x=val_cond,y=drink_rating, fill=binge_drink_cat)) +
  geom_col(position=position_dodge()) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=drink_rating-1.96*se_drink_rating,ymax=drink_rating+1.96*se_drink_rating),position=position_dodge(width=0.9),width=0.2) +
  theme_bw() +
  labs(y='Alcohol Craving',x='Valence Condition',fill='Binge Drinking \nHabits', tag='C')+
  scale_fill_manual(values=c('lightgrey','darkgrey'))+ theme(legend.position='bottom')

ggarrange(valPlot1,valPlot2,bingePlot, ncol=2, nrow=2)

@

Furthermore, in Study 2, we conducted a specificity test, examining whether our stimuli manipulated alcohol craving as opposed to the general urge to consume beverages. We found a significant interaction between valence and cue type (whether participants rated their urge to consume alcoholic or non-alcoholic beverages per trial). Craving for non-alcoholic beverages was lower after viewing pro- compared to anti-alcohol messages (Table \ref{tab:CravingTable}, Figure \ref{fig:cravingPlots}B). 

<<echo=FALSE>>=
pc_source1a=read_csv('Tables/craving_table_pairwiseComps_source1a.csv',col_types=cols())
pc_source1b=read_csv('Tables/craving_table_pairwiseComps_source1b.csv',col_types=cols())
pc_source2=read_csv('Tables/craving_table_pairwiseComps_source2.csv',col_types=cols())

pc_source1a=pc_source1a %>% filter(contrast=='(peer-produced) - professional') %>% mutate(estimate=paste0('estimate = ', round(estimate,2), ', SE= ', round(SE,2), ', p ', formatp(p.value))) %>% .$estimate
pc_source1b=pc_source1b %>% filter(contrast=='(peer-produced) - professional') %>% mutate(estimate=paste0('estimate = ', round(estimate,2), ', SE= ', round(SE,2), ', p ', formatp(p.value))) %>% .$estimate
pc_source2=pc_source2 %>% filter(contrast=='(peer-produced) - professional') %>% mutate(estimate=paste0('estimate = ', round(estimate,2), ', SE= ', round(SE,2), ', p ', formatp(p.value))) %>% .$estimate
@

Using emmeans, we found no main effects of source across all three studies (Study 1a: $\Sexpr{pc_source1a}$, Study 1b: $\Sexpr{pc_source1b}$, Study 2: $\Sexpr{pc_source2}$) and Table \ref{tab:CravingTable} shows no evidence of interactions between source and valence.

\subsubsection{3.1.1 Drinking habits and message receptivity}

A possible explanation for the ineffectiveness of anti-alcohol (vs. non-alcoholic) messages is that anti-alcohol messages often target high risk (binge) drinkers and may not resonate with low risk drinkers. Yet, an additional multi-level regression model fit to Study 1b data (Figure \ref{fig:cravingPlots}C, Supplementary Table 5) shows that those who reported higher binge drinking frequency, on average, reported higher alcohol craving and a higher sensitivity to pro-alcohol (vs. non-alcoholic), but not to anti-alcohol messages.

\subsection{3.2 RQ2: Mechanisms}

Next, we examined the extent to which different message types succeeded in engaging emotional responses and relatability as well as differences across message types in the relationship between each mechanism and alcohol craving.

\subsubsection{3.2.1 Emotions}

<<emoTablesAvg,echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
EmoAvgneg=read_csv('Tables/EmoAvgneg_table.csv',col_types=cols())
EmoAvgpos=read_csv('Tables/EmoAvgpos_table.csv',col_types=cols())

study1a_emo_df=readRDS('data/study1a/cleaned/emo_df.RDS') %>% mutate(Study='Study 1a')
study1b_emo_df=readRDS('data/study1b/cleaned/emo_df.RDS') %>% mutate(Study='Study 1b') %>% mutate(emo_rating=as.numeric(emo_rating))

emo_1ab=bind_rows(study1a_emo_df,study1b_emo_df) %>% pivot_wider(names_from=specific_emotion,values_from=emo_rating) %>%
  mutate(val_cond=case_when(grepl('non',condition)~'non-alcoholic',
                            grepl('anti',condition)~'anti-alcohol',
                            grepl('pro',condition)~'pro-alcohol',
                            TRUE~NA_character_),
         source_cond=case_when(grepl('social',condition)~'peer',
                               grepl('prof',condition)~'professional',
                               TRUE~NA_character_))

@

To examine differences in emotionality across message types in Studies 1a/b, we fit two multi-level regression models regressing negative and positive emotions, respectively, on valence, source, and their interaction, accounting for random intercepts across participants, stimuli and studies. Pro-alcohol messages were associated with more positive ($\Sexpr{EmoAvgpos$Estimate[2]}$) and less negative ($\Sexpr{EmoAvgneg$Estimate[2]}$) emotional responses than anti-alcohol messages. Professionally produced messages evoked more negative emotions than peer-produced messages ($\Sexpr{EmoAvgneg$Estimate[3]}$), which was qualified by an interaction with valence ($\Sexpr{EmoAvgneg$Estimate[4]}$) such that the source effect was only present for anti-alcohol messages. Figure ~\ref{fig:FamEmoFigure}A further shows no substantial differences in the overall emotionality of messages varying in valence and source. For instance, the strength of negative responses to anti-alcohol messages (Mean=$\Sexpr{round(mean(emo_1ab$negative[grepl('anti',emo_1ab$condition)],na.rm=T),2)}$, SD=$\Sexpr{round(sd(emo_1ab$negative[grepl('anti',emo_1ab$condition)],na.rm=T),2)}$) is roughly matched by the strength of positive responses to pro-alcohol messages (Mean =$\Sexpr{round(mean(emo_1ab$positive[grepl('pro',emo_1ab$condition)],na.rm=T),2)}$, SD=$\Sexpr{round(sd(emo_1ab$positive[grepl('pro',emo_1ab$condition)],na.rm=T),2)}$).

<<MakeEmoFigureOne, echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
drink_1ab=study1a_drink_df %>% bind_rows(study1b_drink_df)
emo_vars=unique(study1a_emo_df$specific_emotion) 
emo_vars=case_when(grepl('embarrassed',emo_vars)~sub('\\(','\n \\(',emo_vars),TRUE~emo_vars)

emo_df_1ab=study1a_emo_df %>% bind_rows(study1b_emo_df) %>% left_join(drink_1ab) 

fig_df=emo_df_1ab %>%
mutate(source=case_when(source=grepl('prof',condition)~'professional',
                          grepl('soc',condition)~'peer-produced',
                          TRUE~NA_character_),
         valence=case_when(grepl('non_',condition)~'non-alcoholic',
                           grepl('pro_',condition)~'pro-alcohol',
                           grepl('anti_',condition)~'anti-alcohol',
                           TRUE~NA_character_),
         condition=case_when(condition=='pro_social'~'pro-alcohol \npeer-produced',
                             condition=='pro_prof'~'pro-alcohol \nprofessional',
                             condition=='anti_social'~'anti-alcohol \npeer-produced',
                             condition=='anti_prof'~'anti-alcohol \nprofessional',
                             condition=='non_pro_prof'~'non-alcoholic \nprofessional',
                             condition=='non_pro_social'~'non-alcoholic \npeer-produced',
                             TRUE~NA_character_),
         specific_emotion=paste0(specific_emotion, ' emotions')) %>% 
  group_by(valence,source,specific_emotion,filename) %>% 
  summarize(mean_emo_rating=mean(emo_rating,na.rm=T),
            mean_drink_rating=mean(drink_rating,na.rm=T)) 
  
fig_emo1=fig_df%>%
  group_by(valence,source,specific_emotion) %>%
  summarize(`Average Emotion Rating (1-7)`=mean(mean_emo_rating,na.rm=T),
            se_emo=std.error(mean_emo_rating,na.rm=T)) %>% 
filter(!is.na(source)) %>% 
mutate(valence=case_when(valence=='anti-alcohol'~'anti',
valence=='pro-alcohol'~'pro',
valence=='non-alcoholic'~'non',
TRUE~valence)) %>%
ggplot(aes(x=valence,y=`Average Emotion Rating (1-7)`,fill=source)) + 
    geom_bar(stat='identity',position='dodge') +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=`Average Emotion Rating (1-7)`-(1.96*se_emo),ymax=`Average Emotion Rating (1-7)`+(1.96*se_emo)),position=position_dodge(0.9),width=0.15) +
  facet_wrap(~specific_emotion) +
  scale_fill_manual(values=c('lightgrey','darkgrey')) +
  labs(x='Valence Condition',fill='Source',tag='A') + theme_bw() + theme(legend.position='bottom')
@

Table ~\ref{tab:emofamTable} (left) shows relationships between emotional responses to message exposure and alcohol craving by message conditions in Studies 1a/b. On average, positive emotional responses to alcohol-related messages increased alcohol craving and negative emotional responses decreased alcohol craving. Further confirming H1a, positive emotions were more strongly positively associated with craving for pro- vs. anti-alcohol messages. Yet, targeted emotions did not universally act as stronger pathways towards craving across all messages. In contrast to H1b, the negative effect of negative emotional responses on craving was also stronger for pro- compared to anti-alcohol messages (Figure \ref{fig:FamEmoFigure}B). Emotional responses did not interact with source.

