@

<<emofamTable, echo=FALSE,message=FALSE,results='asis'>>=
emo_fam_table=read_csv('Tables/emoFamTable.csv',col_types=cols()) 

emo_fam_table[is.na(emo_fam_table)]<-''

emo_fam_table %>% kable(caption='Relationship between grand mean-centered positive and negative emotions in response to alcohol-related messages ("Emotion Effect") and perceived relatability of the messages ("Relatability Effect") and alcohol craving in Studies 1a/b by message type)',booktabs=T) %>% 
kable_styling(latex_options = "scale_down") %>% add_footnote("pID = participant ID; sID = stimulus ID; Trials displaying non-alcoholic stimuli in Study 1b are excluded from these analyses. Emotion model equation: alcohol craving ~ positive emotions * valence + negative emotions * valence + positive emotions * source + negative emotions * source + (1|participant) + (1|stimulus) + (1|study); Relatability model equation: alcohol craving ~ relatability * valence + relatability * source + (1|participant) + (1|stimulus) + (1|study)")
@

\subsubsection{3.2.2 Relatability}

<<echo=FALSE, message=FALSE>>=
familiarity_study1ab=study1a_familiarity_df %>% bind_rows(study1b_familiarity_df) %>% 
  mutate(var=case_when(grepl('like me',familiarity_variable)~'like me',
                       grepl('familiar with',familiarity_variable)~'familiar situation',
                       grepl('imagine seeing',familiarity_variable)~'realisitc',
                       grepl('high quality',familiarity_variable)~'high quality',
                       TRUE~NA_character_),
         source=case_when(grepl('prof',condition)~'professional',
                          grepl('soc',condition)~'peer-produced',
                          TRUE~NA_character_),
         valence=case_when(grepl('non_',condition)~'non-alcoholic',
                           grepl('pro_',condition)~'pro-alcohol',
                           grepl('anti_',condition)~'anti-alcohol',
                           TRUE~NA_character_),
         condition=case_when(condition=='pro_social'~'pro-alcohol \npeer-produced',
                             condition=='pro_prof'~'pro-alcohol \nprofessional',
                             condition=='anti_social'~'anti-alcohol \npeer-produced',
                             condition=='anti_prof'~'anti-alcohol \nprofessional',
                             condition=='non_pro_prof'~'non-alcoholic \nprofessional',
                             condition=='non_pro_social'~'non-alcoholic \npeer-produced',
                             TRUE~NA_character_)) %>%
  dplyr::select(-familiarity_variable)

familiarity_study1ab = familiarity_study1ab %>% 
  pivot_wider(values_from=fam_rating,names_from=var) %>% 
  mutate(fam_index=(`like me` + `familiar situation`)/2)

famAvg=read_csv('Tables/famAvg_table.csv',col_types=cols())
@

To test average condition differences in perceived relatability, we fit a multi-level regression model to Study 1a/b data regressing relatability on valence, source, and their interaction, accounting for random intercepts across participants, stimuli and studies. Confirming H2, pro-alcohol messages were perceived as more relatable than anti-alcohol messages ($\Sexpr{famAvg$Estimate[2]}$), with pro-alcohol messages being almost as relatable as non-alcoholic information (Figure \ref{fig:FamEmoFigure}C). We did not find significant effects of source or a source by valence interaction.

<<FamFigureOne, echo=FALSE,message=FALSE>>=
fig_fam1=familiarity_study1ab%>%
  dplyr::group_by(source,valence,filename) %>%
    summarize(fam_index=mean(fam_index,na.rm=T)) %>% 
  group_by(source,valence) %>%
  summarize(`Average Rating (1-7)`=mean(fam_index,na.rm=T),se_fam_index=std.error(fam_index,na.rm=T)) %>% ggplot(aes(valence,`Average Rating (1-7)`,fill=source)) + 
  geom_bar(stat='identity',position='dodge') + labs(x='Valence',y='Relatability Index (1-7)', fill='Source',tag='C') +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin=`Average Rating (1-7)`-(1.96*se_fam_index),ymax=`Average Rating (1-7)`+(1.96*se_fam_index)),position=position_dodge(0.9),width=0.15)+
  scale_fill_manual(values=c('lightgrey','darkgrey')) + theme_bw()+ theme(legend.position='bottom')
@

Table \ref{tab:emofamTable} (right) shows relationships between message relatability and alcohol craving by message conditions in Studies 1a/b. In partial support of H3, relatability enhanced the intended effects of pro-, but not anti-alcohol messages. Specifically, relatability was more strongly positively associated with alcohol craving in pro- compared to anti-alcohol messages. However, the effect of relatability on alcohol craving remained positive in response to anti-alcohol messages (Figure \ref{fig:FamEmoFigure}D). Again, we did not find source by relatability interactions.

<<FamEmoFigure, echo=FALSE,message=FALSE,results='asis',fig.cap='Drivers of Alcohol Craving in Studies 1a/b. A. Emotional responses by message type, B. Relationship between negative and positive emotional responses and alcohol craving, C. Perceived familiarity by message type, D. Relationship between perceived relatability and alcohol craving. Error bars in A and C represent standard errors.'>>=

familiarity_1ab=study1a_familiarity_df %>% bind_rows(study1b_familiarity_df)

familiarity_1ab_wide = familiarity_1ab %>% 
  pivot_wider(values_from=fam_rating,names_from=familiarity_variable) %>% 
  mutate(fam_index=(`shows people that are like me`+`shows a situation I'm familiar with`)/2)

fig_fam2=drink_1ab %>% right_join(familiarity_1ab_wide) %>% 
  group_by(filename) %>%
  summarise(fam_index=mean(fam_index,na.rm=T),
            drink_rating=mean(drink_rating,na.rm=T),
            val_cond=first(val_cond),
            source_cond=first(source_cond)) %>% 
  mutate(val_cond=factor(val_cond,levels=c('anti-alcohol','non-alcoholic','pro-alcohol'))) %>%
  filter(val_cond!='non-alcoholic') %>% 
  ggplot(aes(x=fam_index,y=drink_rating,color=val_cond)) + 
  geom_smooth(method='lm') +
  geom_point(alpha=0.3) +
  theme_bw() +
  scale_color_manual(values=c('black','darkgrey')) +
  ylab('Alcohol Craving') + 
  xlab('Relatability Index') +
  labs(color='Valence',tag='D')+ theme(legend.position='bottom')

# Arrange Plots
plot_row1 <- ggarrange(plotlist = list(fig_emo1,fig_fam1), ncol = 2, common.legend = TRUE,legend='bottom')
plot_row2 <- ggarrange(plotlist = list(fig_emo2,fig_fam2), ncol = 2, common.legend = TRUE,legend='bottom')

ggarrange(plot_row1, plot_row2, nrow = 2)
@

\section{4. Discussion}

Within rich, dynamic information environments, persuasive messages are in acute competition with thematically related messages encountered in close spatial and temporal proximity. Competitors may be congruent or conflicting in content and source. In the context of alcohol, young adults navigate a competitive landscape shaped by alcohol brands and health officials, partygoing peers and concerned parents. What message prevails when a professional anti-alcohol ad competes with a peer's party pictures, and why? Across three studies, we asked: What are the relative effects of alcohol-related messages varying in valence and source on alcohol craving? Are these effects driven by similar or different psychological mechanisms across message types?

\subsection{4.1 Pro-Alcohol messages are more impactful than anti-Alcohol messages}

We found that pro-alcohol messages substantially increased craving compared to non-alcoholic and anti-alcohol messages. Anti-alcohol messages were significantly less impactful than pro-alcohol messages and did not reduce craving relative to non-alcoholic messages. This adds to sparse and inconsistent previous findings on whether pro- \parencite{stautz_impact_2017} or anti-alcohol \parencite{stautz_viewing_2016} messages are more effective.

We discuss three explanations. First, anti-alcohol messages may inherently be less impactful than pro-alcohol messaging. Although not explicitly tested in prior work, this aligns with reviews identifying small or even statistically insignificant effects of anti-alcohol campaigns \parencite{young_effectiveness_2018} and stronger effects of alcohol marketing on consumption \parencite{jernigan_alcohol_2017}. Here, we confirm these independent observations through a direct comparison of message types, using a large, diverse sample of messages that is substantially more representative of the general information environment than average stimulus sets in persuasion research. Future research may extend this work by systematically examining the role of specific message characteristics (e.g., comparing pro- and anti-alcohol messaging focused on social vs. health impacts of alcohol).

Second, our results may reflect a context-driven floor effect. Baseline alcohol craving was already low in response to non-alcoholic messages (M = $\Sexpr{study1b_drink_df %>% filter(grepl('non',condition)) %>% summarize(mean=round(mean(drink_rating),2),sd=round(sd(drink_rating),2)) %>% pull(mean)}$, SD = $\Sexpr{study1b_drink_df %>% filter(grepl('non',condition)) %>% summarize(mean=round(mean(drink_rating),2),sd=round(sd(drink_rating),2)) %>% pull(sd)}$ on a scale from 1-7), leaving limited room for further reduction by anti-alcohol messages. The message exposure contexts of our online samples in Study 1a/b plausibly resembles a natural exposure context for alcohol-related content (browsing the internet on your own time). Assuming that non-addicted individuals exhibit low alcohol craving most of the time, anti-alcohol messages may have a substantially smaller window of opportunity for impacting craving compared to pro-alcohol messages. Further, high craving situations may be further complicated by the presence of conflicting pro-alcohol messages (e.g., pro-alcohol comments from friends). 

Third, most anti-alcohol campaigns target extreme drinking rather than advocating abstinence. We thus tested whether alcohol-related messages resonated more with frequent binge drinkers. On the contrary, more frequent binge drinkers were more sensitive to pro-, but not to anti-alcohol messaging. This partially aligns with earlier findings that heavier drinkers perceive pro-alcohol content as more effective and anti-alcohol content as less effective \parencite{austin_assessing_1999}. Overall, these results suggest that restricting pro-alcohol content may be more effective than promoting anti-alcohol messaging.

\subsection{4.2 Psychological mechanisms disproportionately support pro-alcohol message effects}

Next, we examined to what extent the message types included here relied on overlapping or distinct psychological mechanisms to influence craving. Mechanistic overlap may increase the chance of interference (i.e., amplification or suppression effects) when messages are in acute competition. Specifically, we examined the relative success of each message type in engaging emotional and relatability responses and compared the relevance of each mechanism in determining craving across message types. Extending prior findings \parencite{stautz_impact_2017} to more diverse messages, we found that pro-alcohol messages elicited more positive and less negative emotions than anti-alcohol messages. In contrast to expectations proposed by prior work \parencite{austin_assessing_1999}, the average strength of emotionality was comparable across pro- and anti-alcohol messages. We further found an interaction between valence and source such that professional anti-alcohol messages elicited stronger negative emotions than peer-produced anti-alcohol messaging, whereas emotional responses to pro-alcohol messaging varied little across source. This is in line with Benign Violation Theory \parencite{mcgraw_benign_2010} which suggests that norm violations like overconsumption of alcohol elicit amusement if perceived as non-threatening. Pro-alcohol social norms likely discourage strong anti-alcohol sentiments in peer-produced content, which may instead put a positive spin on negative emotions associated with adverse drinking outcomes. Such ambiguity, most commonly studied in science communication, is generally negatively associated with persuasiveness \parencite{gustafson_review_2020}, partially because ambiguous messages can be discounted more easily \parencite{keating_ambiguity_2023}. 

Furthermore, in line with Becheur and Valette-Florence (\citeyear{becheur_use_2014}), we found that positive emotions increased while negative emotions decreased craving. Importantly, we also directly compared these relationships across message types and found universally stronger emotion-craving links for pro-compared to anti-alcohol messages, suggesting that craving is more emotionally driven in response to pro-alcohol messages. This disparity could stem from the strong pro-alcohol norms among young adults. In this context, negative emotions in response to anti-alcohol content may create dissonance \parencite{festinger_theory_1957}, motivating down-regulation of uncomfortable emotions \parencite{agrawal_emotional_2010,dore_brain_2019}. This may selectively weaken the impact of emotional responses to anti-alcohol but not pro-alcohol messaging. More comparative research is needed to understand these mechanisms and related implications. For instance, will emotional anti-alcohol appeals always be overpowered by emotional pro-alcohol appeals in acute competitive scenarios? And what is the role of specific discrete emotions?

Our results further show that participants found pro-alcohol content substantially more relatable than anti-alcohol content. Peer-produced pro-alcohol content was as relatable as non-alcoholic content. This aligns with prior work showing that anti-alcohol content is relatively rare online \parencite{hendriks_alcohol-related_2017} and offline \parencite{scholz_neural_2019}, partially because pro-alcohol sentiments are incentivised by social norms \parencite{borsari_peer_2001,duckworth_transitions_2021}. Relatability enhanced the effectiveness of pro-alcohol messages, consistent with prior research \parencite{cosme_message_2022,kim_effect_2016,lustria_meta-analysis_2013}. Surprisingly, relatability of anti-alcohol messages did not support persuasive effects and, instead, also enhanced craving, although this effect was significantly weaker. This difference may further explain the finding that frequent binge drinkers showed heightened sensitivity to pro- but not anti-alcohol information. Higher frequency drinking may be associated with higher overall relatability, which is in turn more strongly linked to craving for pro-compared to anti-alcohol messages.

\subsection{4.3 Minimal source effects}

Strikingly, message source had limited impact across analyses. Professionally designed content did not outperform peer-produced content, even though peers were unknown rather than close others who would likely exert larger influence \parencite{tajfel_integrative_1979}. This stands in contrast to findings that professional production quality enhances persuasiveness \parencite{amos_exploring_2008}. One explanation is that peer-produced messaging compensates for lower production quality through greater relatability and authenticity. Prior work (largely outside the context of health messaging) supports authenticity as a key persuasive factor \parencite{petraglia_importance_2009,zheng_not_2024}, especially on social media \parencite{poyry_call_2021}, where users may be desensitized to variance in production quality through frequent exposures to peer-produced messaging, thus emphasizing other aspects like authenticity \parencite{lawrence_when_2013}. It has even been suggested that lower production quality can enhance the persuasiveness of user generated content by signaling trustworthiness \parencite{hautz_let_2014}. Furthermore, as discussed earlier, a feature of peer-produced content that may limit persuasiveness is ambiguity introduced by ill defined or absent persuasive intent. Here, we may have artificially reduced ambiguity by selecting content with clear valence. More comparative work is needed to understand the boundary conditions of source effects, for instance exploring the role of social closeness or brand attitudes, especially given the current lack of related work in health communication \parencite{jenkins_assessing_2020}.

\subsection{4.4 Limitations}

First, alcohol craving is known to vary depending on individual differences (e.g., cultural context, drinking habits) and time (e.g., time of day or weekday). Future work should examine the role of these contextual variables in acute message competition. Second, although our set of 190 message stimuli is more representative of the alcohol information environment than many comparable studies, it does not cover all message types and we may have further limited generalizability to other situations by enforcing a controlled experimental design (e.g., within-subject design with an alcohol-only environment rather than a more diverse information environment, disambiguated message conditions). Third, we examined a relatability index, averaging perceived similarity with depicted characters and familiarity with depicted scenarios. Future work may add nuance by examining sub-dimensions of relatability separately.

\subsection{4.5 Conclusions and future directions}

This report conceptualized acute message competition as spatially and/or temporally proximal exposure to thematically related messages, distinguishing it from existing work on cumulative exposure to related messages over long time periods. Empirically, we identified a context in which acute message competition may limit the effectiveness of health communication: anti-alcohol messages underperformed in direct comparison to pro-alcohol messages in a context with low baseline craving.

As such, this report raises an important question for persuasion research: In what situations does acute message competition occur and in what way? Key next steps include testing specific order effects (e.g., pro-alcohol messages following anti-alcohol messages or vice versa) in ecologically valid contexts such as natural(istic) social media feeds containing related message types in situations where alcohol craving is high or low. Our findings generate testable hypotheses, including that effects of anti-alcohol messages may be suppressed in direct competition with pro-alcohol messages, particularly when both operate through emotional or relatability mechanisms. 

Methodologically, studying acute message competition requires novel paradigms for evaluating multiple messages per participant. This raises challenges of participant burden and bias, especially in examining order effects. A key decision involves selecting outcome variables: relatively stable constructs (e.g., attitudes) may be less vulnerable to acute message competition than dynamic states (e.g., craving). Implicit measures, such as psychophysiological indicators, may reduce burden and capture effects more sensitively.

Theoretically, if order effects are substantiated, a broader framework of acute message competition across persuasive contexts will be needed. Such a framework, in fruitful interaction with other state-of-the-art approaches such as just-in-time interventions, could identify conditions that mitigate suppression effects (where an intended message effect disappears in a competitive scenario) or support amplification effects (where an intended message effect is amplified by the presence of other messages), ultimately enhancing the overall effectiveness of public health communication.

